+This family computes geometric attributes (normals, area) or improves visual quality (smoothing) of isosurfaces extracted from scalar fields [G]. It addresses limitations of raw isosurface extraction, such as jagged artifacts in binary data [G]. It differs from its siblings, which focus on mesh topology and connectivity [30-isosurface-extraction.md], by operating on the resulting geometry or the input field to refine surface representation [G].
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on the output of an isosurface extraction algorithm or the underlying scalar field [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md, 1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md, 1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md]. The core mechanism involves evaluating local geometric properties or applying transformations to the scalar data to influence the final mesh [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md, 1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md, 1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md].
-1. **Geometric Evaluation**: For normals and area, the method evaluates the local geometry of the generated mesh primitives (triangles) or the underlying scalar field gradients [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md, 1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md].
-2. **Field Transformation**: For smoothing, the method transforms the input scalar field (e.g., binary to continuous signed distance) before extraction to ensure a smoother isosurface [1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md].
-3. **Post-Processing or Pre-Processing**: Methods are categorized by whether they modify the mesh after extraction (normals, area) or the input data before extraction (smoothing) [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md, 1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md, 1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md].
