 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Marching Cubes Surface Normal Estimation](1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md)
-Computes surface normals for an isosurface mesh by approximating the gradient of the underlying scalar field at vertex positions using central differences, then normalizing the result [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md]. Differs from geometric normal estimation by relying on the analytic properties of the scalar field, providing smoother normals that reflect underlying data density rather than just discretized mesh topology [1005-marching-cubes-surface-normal-estimation.md]. Choose when accurate lighting calculations are required and the scalar field is accessible.
+Computes surface normals for an isosurface mesh by approximating the gradient of the underlying scalar field at vertex positions using central differences, then normalizing the result. Differs from geometric normal estimation by relying on the analytic properties of the scalar field, providing smoother normals that reflect underlying data density rather than just discretized mesh topology. Choose when accurate lighting calculations are required and the scalar field is accessible.
 
 ### [Marching Cubes Surface Area Estimation](1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md)
-Estimates the surface area of a solid by summing the areas of the triangular polygons produced by the marching cubes algorithm [1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md]. Differs from siblings by focusing on the aggregate geometric measure (area) rather than local vector properties or topological smoothing [1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md]. Choose when quantitative analysis of surface extent is needed. Note that standard linear implementations lack multigrid convergence for certain orientations [1315-marching-cubes-surface-area-estimation.md].
+Estimates the surface area of a solid by summing the areas of the triangular polygons produced by the marching cubes algorithm. Differs from siblings by focusing on the aggregate geometric measure (area) rather than local vector properties or topological smoothing. Choose when quantitative analysis of surface extent is needed. Note that standard linear implementations lack multigrid convergence for certain orientations.
 
 ### [Marching Cubes Smoothing](1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md)
-Converts a binary volume embedding into a continuous signed scalar field to eliminate jagged, stair-stepped artifacts inherent in extracting isosurfaces directly from discrete masks [1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md]. Differs from siblings by operating on the input scalar field prior to extraction rather than post-processing the resulting mesh geometry [1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md]. Preserves topological information of the original binary embedding without eroding fine details [1594-marching-cubes-smoothing.md]. Choose when extracting surfaces from binary masks to avoid low-quality meshes.
